class Solution {
    public int search(int[] nums, int target) {

        // Initialize search boundaries
        int low = 0;
        int high = nums.length - 1;

        // Standard binary search loop
        while (low <= high) {

            // Calculate mid to avoid integer overflow
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            // If target found, return its index
            if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;

            // Check if the LEFT HALF is sorted
            if (nums[low] <= nums[mid]) {

                // If target lies inside the left sorted half
                // Range: nums[low] → nums[mid]
                if (target >= nums[low] && target < nums[mid]) {

                    // Search in left half
                    high = mid - 1;

                } else {

                    // Target is not in left half
                    // Move to right half
                    low = mid + 1;
                }
            }

            // Otherwise RIGHT HALF must be sorted
            else {

                // If target lies inside the right sorted half
                // Range: nums[mid] → nums[high]
                if (target > nums[mid] && target <= nums[high]) {

                    // Search in right half
                    low = mid + 1;

                } else {

                    // Target is not in right half
                    // Move to left half
                    high = mid - 1;
                }
            }
        }

        // Target not found
        return -1;
    }
}

🧠 How the Algorithm Works (Short Idea)

A rotated sorted array always has one sorted half.

Example:

[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]

At any mid:

Either left side is sorted

Or right side is sorted

So we:

Detect which side is sorted.

Check if the target lies in that sorted range.

Move the search accordingly.

✅ Time Complexity: O(log n) (binary search)
✅ Space Complexity: O(1)
